item.text:
I searched official Chinese personnel records, contemporaneous diplomatic readouts, UN sanctions materials, U.S. enforcement records, government-sponsored strategic analysis, and nuclear/biorisk review material. The searches mostly converged on already-known evidence.

The strongest additions are: a precise contemporaneous locator upgrading WD008; Xi’s documented promotion of the 2022 Rocket Force commander; the post-congress CMC roster; an independent analysis characterizing the Taiwan exercises as conventional coercive deterrence without establishing nuclear cover; and an enforcement record separating DPRK operatives in China from Chinese state action.

Unresolved: no independently audited evidence surfaced for 2022 military-nuclear custody or accident-prevention implementation; no authoritative source tied Xi’s Taiwan exercise decision to nuclear cover; no evidence established professional autonomy of nuclear advisers or tested crisis-message performance; and no independent 2022 audit of China’s military-AI, cyber, biological, or other catastrophic-risk safeguards was found. These remain confidence-limiting gaps.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"President Xi Jinping Meets with German Chancellor Olaf Scholz","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/eng/gjhdq_665435/3265_665445/3296_664550/3298_664554/202211/t20221104_10800546.html","claim":"A contemporaneous Chinese readout states that Xi Jinping advocated opposing the threat or use of nuclear weapons, maintaining that nuclear weapons could not be used and nuclear war must not be fought, and preventing a Eurasian nuclear crisis. This upgrades WD008's later retrospective account but remains evidence of Xi's declared diplomatic position, not domestic implementation.","locator":"Dated heading and paragraphs 69 and 91–92, especially paragraph 92's four-part Ukraine-crisis prescription","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C2-001","canonical_fact_key":"xi_scholz_2022_nuclear_nonuse_statement","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.2","1B.5"],"source_type":"contemporaneous_official_diplomatic_readout","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary contemporaneous government record precisely identifies Xi, the meeting date, and the nuclear-risk statement; it cannot independently establish implementation or private bilateral commitments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly records Xi's conduct on 4 November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: the readout expressly attributes the position to President Xi in his meeting with Scholz.","contrary_evidence":"The statement did not criticize Russia by name, establish enforcement, or show that the same restraint governed Taiwan-related military conduct.","underlying_fact_key":"xi_scholz_2022_nuclear_nonuse_statement","lenses":["1B.2","1B.5"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Central Military Commission holds ceremony for promotion to general; Xi Jinping presents promotion orders","publisher":"Cyberspace Administration of China, reproducing CCTV","publication_date":"2022-01-21","url":"https://www.cac.gov.cn/2022-01/21/c_1644368249559053.htm","claim":"Xi, acting as CMC chairman, signed and personally presented the promotion order naming PLA Rocket Force commander Li Yuchao among seven officers promoted to full general. This establishes direct Xi-level ownership of a consequential 2022 nuclear-command personnel decision, but supplies no evidence about technical qualifications, adviser independence, or appointment performance.","locator":"Headline and paragraphs/HTML lines 44–56, especially lines 50–54 identifying Xi's signed order and Li Yuchao as Rocket Force commander","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C2-002","canonical_fact_key":"xi_li_yuchao_rocket_force_promotion_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.7"],"source_type":"official_personnel_decision","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official record names the officeholder, decision mechanism, date, and Xi's personal role; it is authoritative for the appointment action but not its quality.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The promotion order and ceremony occurred on 21 January 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: Xi signed and presented the CMC promotion order.","contrary_evidence":"The ceremonial record offers no evidence of professional autonomy, crisis competence, safety expertise, or independent appointment review.","underlying_fact_key":"xi_li_yuchao_rocket_force_promotion_2022","lenses":["1B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Communique of the First Plenary Session of the 20th Central Committee of the Communist Party of China","publisher":"Xinhua, reproduced by the Supreme People's Court of China","publication_date":"2022-10-24","url":"https://english.court.gov.cn/2022-10/24/c_823880.htm","claim":"The October 2022 plenary session, presided over by Xi, named Xi chairman of a seven-person Central Military Commission, with Zhang Youxia and He Weidong as vice chairmen and Li Shangfu, Liu Zhenli, Miao Hua, and Zhang Shengmin as members. The record identifies the formal top command team but reveals no dedicated nuclear technical adviser, appointment criteria, dissent channel, or professional-autonomy safeguard.","locator":"Lines 138–147 describing the session and Xi's role; lines 165–170 listing the complete CMC roster","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C2-003","canonical_fact_key":"china_cmc_roster_october_2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.7"],"source_type":"official_party_appointment_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary contemporaneous roster is authoritative for formal appointments, but institutional silence cannot prove that advisers or autonomy mechanisms did not exist elsewhere.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Records the command structure selected on 23 October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Xi presided over the session and was named CMC chairman; the source does not specify his personal authorship of every membership choice.","contrary_evidence":"The existence of multiple CMC members could distribute advice, but this roster alone does not demonstrate their autonomy or nuclear-crisis functions.","underlying_fact_key":"china_cmc_roster_october_2022","lenses":["1B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The 'vicious diplomacy' of coercion: China's military exercises in the Taiwan Strait","publisher":"Australian Department of Defence","publication_date":"2022-08","url":"https://www.defence.gov.au/defence-activities/research-innovation/research-publications/vicious-diplomacy-coercion-chinas-military-exercises-taiwan-strait","claim":"Independent strategic analysis characterized the 3–10 August PLA exercises as coercive diplomacy intended to deter Taiwan and the United States and compel movement toward Beijing's reunification objective. The analysis describes conventional military threat and coercive deterrence, identifies heightened miscalculation and escalation risks, but does not identify nuclear deployment, nuclear signaling, or nuclear cover. It therefore supports a cautious separation between the exercises' evidenced coercive purpose and an unproven nuclear-cover interpretation.","locator":"Publication summary lines 9–14; especially lines 11–14 on coercive diplomacy, deterrence/compellence, intended audiences, and escalation risk","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C2-004","canonical_fact_key":"august_2022_taiwan_exercises_conventional_coercive_deterrence","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.5"],"source_type":"independent_government_published_strategic_analysis","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Named expert analysis published contemporaneously by an Australian government research outlet clearly supports the coercive-deterrence interpretation; absence of a nuclear finding is probative only for careful separation, not proof that nuclear considerations were absent from classified planning.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in August 2022 and directly analyzes the 3–10 August 2022 exercises.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual: the conduct was by the PLA under Xi's ultimate CMC authority, but the source does not establish Xi's personal exercise order or planning instructions.","contrary_evidence":"The analysis finds increased pathways to miscalculation and escalation; conventional characterization does not exclude undisclosed nuclear considerations.","underlying_fact_key":"august_2022_taiwan_exercises_conventional_coercive_deterrence","lenses":["1B.5"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Treasury Targets Democratic People's Republic of Korea Individuals Supporting Weapons of Mass Destruction and Ballistic Missile Programs","publisher":"United States Department of the Treasury","publication_date":"2022-01-12","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy0555","claim":"OFAC designated four DPRK nationals based in Dalian or Shenyang as representatives of DPRK weapons-procurement organizations. Treasury alleged that they procured steel alloys, software, chemicals, and other goods, in some cases from Chinese companies. The action documents proliferation activity occurring from Chinese territory but expressly identifies the actors as DPRK representatives; it does not find that Xi or the Chinese state directed the procurement.","locator":"Lines 601–615, especially the 'DPRK WMD Representatives' section at lines 609–614; sanctions consequences at lines 616–619","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C2-005","canonical_fact_key":"dprk_wmd_procurement_representatives_in_china_january_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.6"],"source_type":"official_foreign_sanctions_enforcement_decision","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Formal OFAC designation provides named actors, locations, organizational affiliations, and alleged procurement conduct, but it is a unilateral U.S. administrative determination rather than a court judgment or finding against China.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The enforcement decision was issued on 12 January 2022; some underlying assignments began earlier and may have continued into 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Not attributed to Xi or the Chinese state. The record establishes DPRK operatives' presence and alleged dealings with Chinese companies, creating an enforcement-context question only.","contrary_evidence":"The source neither alleges Chinese state sponsorship nor documents a Chinese investigation, tolerance decision, prosecution, or refusal to act.","underlying_fact_key":"dprk_wmd_procurement_representatives_in_china_january_2022","lenses":["1B.6"]}
